# kajianq/sudoers/kajianq-deploy — the KajianQ deploy identity's entire root
# grant, installed by the applier at /etc/sudoers.d/kajianq-deploy (root:root
# 0440) after `visudo -cf` accepts it: a malformed file in sudoers.d can lock
# sudo out of the box entirely, so the parse is a gate in front of the install,
# not a check after it. KajianQ's provision/vps/ carries the same bytes.
#
# The account name is a literal, not a substituted placeholder, on purpose.
# sudoers grants are per-username, so a configurable name would let this grant
# and the CI variable VPS_USER (which the deploy workflow passes as
# KAJIANQ_DEPLOY_USER) disagree — the deploy would then fail with a confusing
# "password is required" instead of a named mismatch. One name, three places
# that must agree, and tests/scripts/vps-hardening.test.mjs fails the build when
# they stop agreeing.
#
# This grant is the bridge between two deploy paths. The shared contract is
# installed at /etc/vps/deploy/kajianq.conf, so the identity may run
# `vps-deploy kajianq` (deploy and `--rollback`) and the four bounded service
# verbs for the units it declares — `kajianq-api.service`. The deploy workflow
# still ships with rsync, so the legacy commands ride the same NOPASSWD line:
# restart the API unit after the rsync, start the session-reclamation oneshot
# once so a rotated env var fails the deploy instead of surfacing at 03:17, and
# two READ-ONLY diagnostics (`nginx -t` validates, `certbot certificates` lists;
# neither writes state). The post-deploy doctor gate (deploy.sh §5) runs the
# shared-box sweep from ahaqqu/homepage as this identity, and without the
# diagnostics the sweep can only warn that the config is unverifiable.
#
# Both sets are temporary. When KajianQ's deploy switches to `vps-deploy
# kajianq`, the four legacy commands are removed; this grant is then the helper
# invocation and the diagnostics every app's grant carries.
#
# Exact commands, absolute binary paths, no wildcards: wildcard arguments in
# sudoers are the classic privilege-escalation footgun. What this grant does NOT
# give the deploy identity: a root shell, `daemon-reload`, unit-file edits, any
# other service or app, or any read of /etc/kajianq/*.env (root:root 0600 — the
# provider keys and the database password stay unreadable from that session).
#
# Stated rather than left implicit: the deploy identity ships the code
# kajianq-api.service executes and may restart that unit, so it is trusted
# equivalently to the serving process. A stronger boundary needs a root-owned
# installer that reads no deploy-supplied scripts and is recorded as ADR-0044's
# revisit trigger, not pretended here.

# The deploy's ssh calls are non-interactive and allocate no pty. Debian's
# default does not set requiretty, but naming the precondition keeps this grant
# from depending on a distro default a later hardening pass could flip on.
Defaults:kajianq-deploy !requiretty

kajianq-deploy ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/vps-deploy kajianq, \
                                    /usr/local/sbin/vps-deploy kajianq --rollback, \
                                    /usr/local/sbin/vps-deploy kajianq start, \
                                    /usr/local/sbin/vps-deploy kajianq stop, \
                                    /usr/local/sbin/vps-deploy kajianq restart, \
                                    /usr/local/sbin/vps-deploy kajianq status, \
                                    /usr/bin/systemctl restart kajianq-api.service, \
                                    /usr/bin/systemctl start kajianq-cron.service, \
                                    /usr/sbin/nginx -t, \
                                    /usr/bin/certbot certificates
